  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday) \nIssued at 145 AM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe eastern Pacific low and remnants of Rachel will be drawn into\nsouthern CA on Sunday with the deepest subtropical moisture\nstaying close to the lower Colorado river basin. PWATs will raise\na bit more in NM, up to 0.75 to 1.0 inch, but with limited\ninstability, most shower and thunderstorm activity will be reliant\non upper level forcing from the low itself, and that will remain \nmostly relegated to the western half of NM with only spotty to \nscattered coverage at that. Temperatures will hover close to \nnormal in the western half of the forecast area while eastern \nplains zones stay above by 5 to 10 degrees in breezy to windy \nsoutherly flow. \n\nA new, separate low then drops down the CA coastline on Monday \nand then rounds southern CA and crosses into neighboring \nsouthwestern states by Tuesday before trekking over the central \nRockies into Wednesday. This cold core low is not shown to be as \ndeep as it was advertised 24 hours ago with some discontinuities \nin the track also appearing in deterministic and \nprobabilistic/ensemble guidance. Not surprisingly, this has large \nconsequences with regards to the fate of any subtropical moisture \nfrom the next tropical storm, Simon. Latest model runs are at \nbattle on whether to draw the moisture northward before a potent \ndry slot arrives, and it appears that the dry slot is winning for \nmuch of the forecast area. The southeastern quarter of NM might be\nable draw some moisture in before getting dry-slotted late \nTuesday, but confidence is lowering in the overall likelihood and \nextent of precipitation. The POPs have been trimmed significantly \nmore, and instead a trend to more fall-like temperatures is \nlooking to be the more likely and reasonable impact, especially \ninto Tuesday and Wednesday as the Pacific front overtakes NM. \nDaytime highs for much of the western half of the forecast area \nare on track to fall 5 to 15 degrees below normal for mid October \non both Tuesday and Wednesday. Overnight lows will turn cooler, \nwith a few western and northern locales getting closer to the \nfreezing mark, but still most areas will remain near to slightly \nabove average into Wednesday night and Thursday morning. Lighter, \ndrier, and cooler northwest flow aloft on Thursday would relax \nmore into Friday with no notable or widespread impacts foreseen.",
